<Project>

  <!--
    Nested at Alembic/PromptHarness/, with its own PromptHarness.slnx rather than joining
    Distiller's, and not a client of Distiller's own code — the same positioning the repo-root
    PromptHarness/ takes relative to Morgana (its own PromptHarness.slnx beside Morgana.slnx),
    which is why this project carries the same name: an instrument pointed AT Distiller, not a
    part of it. Before Alembic/ became a container for Distiller/ and this project, it sat beside
    Alembic/ as a physical sibling named AlembicHarness; the relationship is unchanged, only where
    it lives on disk is.

    What it tests is deliberately narrow and never what the repo-root PromptHarness tests. That one
    boots a real Morgana host and asks "does the domain Alembic produced behave correctly at
    runtime" — Alembic itself can never answer that, has no channel and no way to, and neither does
    this project. This harness stays entirely on Distiller's own side of that boundary: it drives
    IInterviewService with a scripted "domain expert" the same way a client's own typing would, and
    asks "did Distiller's mapping, its interview conduct across passes, and its finalization emit —
    Draft validation, the archive, the migration report — do what they are supposed to do." A
    domain it interviews is a fixture chosen to exercise Alembic's own doctrine, never a product in
    its own right.
  -->

  <!--
    Explicit Sdk.props/Sdk.targets imports, rather than the usual <Project Sdk="..."> shorthand,
    so ImportDirectoryBuildProps below actually takes effect: the shorthand form imports Sdk.props
    (and with it any Directory.Build.props found by walking up) before this file's own
    PropertyGroup is even parsed, which is too late to opt out. Nesting under Alembic/ means that
    walk-up would otherwise reach Alembic's own Directory.Build.props/.targets — the
    Docker-version-file generator among them — simply because this project now sits in a
    subdirectory of Alembic/. That is an accidental coupling this project never had as a physical
    sibling and should not gain as a side effect of the move.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <ImportDirectoryBuildProps>false</ImportDirectoryBuildProps>
    <ImportDirectoryBuildTargets>false</ImportDirectoryBuildTargets>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <Import Project="Sdk.props" Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk" />

  <PropertyGroup>
    <!-- Common Build Settings -->
    <TargetFramework>net10.0</TargetFramework>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
    <ImplicitUsings>enable</ImplicitUsings>
    <LangVersion>latest</LangVersion>
    <OutputType>Exe</OutputType>
    <IsPackable>false</IsPackable>
    <IsTestProject>true</IsTestProject>

    <!-- Version Information -->
    <Version>0.28.0</Version>
    <AssemblyVersion>$(Version)</AssemblyVersion>
    <FileVersion>$(Version)</FileVersion>

    <!-- Package Metadata -->
    <Title>PromptHarness</Title>
    <PackageId>PromptHarness</PackageId>
    <Authors>Marco De Salvo</Authors>
    <Company>Marco De Salvo</Company>
    <Copyright>Marco De Salvo</Copyright>
    <Product>Morgana</Product>
    <PackageLicenseExpression>Apache-2.0</PackageLicenseExpression>
    <PackageProjectUrl>https://github.com/mdesalvo/Morgana</PackageProjectUrl>

    <Description>
    This is the non-regression harness for Alembic itself — the repo-root PromptHarness is for
    Morgana. It drives IInterviewService with a scripted domain expert against fixture domains
    chosen to exercise Alembic's own doctrine, and asserts on what Distiller's mapping, interview
    conduct and finalization emit actually produce. It never tests the runtime behaviour of a
    domain Alembic produced — that question belongs to the repo-root PromptHarness, and Alembic has
    no channel to answer it with in the first place.
    </Description>

    <!--
      Same UserSecretsId as Distiller itself: the harness owns no secrets of its own and reads
      provider, tiers and API keys from the very store that configures the Alembic under test, so
      the suite always runs against whatever LLM Alembic is currently wired to.
    -->
    <UserSecretsId>374228be-4f26-4382-a3ef-7500a0b829dd</UserSecretsId>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="xunit.v3" Version="3.*" />
    <PackageReference Include="xunit.runner.visualstudio" Version="3.*" />
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.NET.Test.Sdk" Version="18.*" />
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Extensions.Hosting" Version="10.0.0" />
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Extensions.Configuration.UserSecrets" Version="10.0.0" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <!-- The one dependency that matters: Distiller's own services, driven in-process. -->
    <ProjectReference Include="..\Distiller\Distiller.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <Content Include="..\Distiller\appsettings.json" Link="appsettings.json" CopyToOutputDirectory="PreserveNewest" />

    <!--
      The shipped Examples domain, as the fixture for everything that starts from a configuration a
      client already runs rather than from an interview — editing above all. Linked from the repo's
      own copy rather than duplicated here, so the domain these tests correct is the same one the
      rest of the repository builds and demonstrates, and cannot quietly drift from it.
    -->
    <Content Include="..\..\Examples\agents.json" Link="examples-agents.json" CopyToOutputDirectory="PreserveNewest" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <Import Project="Sdk.targets" Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk" />

</Project>
